> Whereas sets must be abstract, because they have no empirical
> correlates, events and forks are concrete (in the sense of being
> observable).
> Forks are concrete: I can point to them, pick them up, etc.  Event
> abstract objects are not.

Events can be pointed to, albeit not picked up. Event abstract objects
and fork abstract objects can be pointed to if they're real; the fork
abstract object, if real, can also be picked up.

> As to observability, the gismu list is silent on whether the x2 of
> "zgana" (the observed) is an event or a concrete object.

There isn't such a dichotomy.

> I suspect that concrete objects are meant.

I suspect that physically manifest things (including events and
forks) are meant.

